--! Exact 32-bit integers, for the arithmetic `Int` cannot do.
--!
--! `Int` is a double (`fast-compiler.md` §3.1): exact to 9007199254740991
--! and silently inexact after it. That is the right default and the wrong
--! tool for hashing, checksums, pseudo-random numbers and binary formats,
--! which are specified as arithmetic that WRAPS at 32 bits. Bit operations
--! alone do not get you there, because a 32-bit product can exceed 2^53 —
--! `0x7fffffff * 0x7fffffff` is off by more than a rounding error before
--! anything masks it — so a wrapping multiply cannot be faked on doubles.
--! Elm's own random and hashing libraries splitting every multiply into
--! 16-bit halves by hand is the evidence.
--!
--! `Int32` is that multiply, and the rest of a wrapping 32-bit machine with
--! it. Every function here is TOTAL: every result is a two's-complement
--! 32-bit value, nothing overflows into inexactness, nothing throws, and
--! division by zero is `zero` rather than an exception or an infinity —
--! the same answer `Basics.idiv`, `Int.mod` and `Int.rem` give.
--!
--! **`Int32` is not in the prelude** (`language.md` Appendix A). Write
--! `import Int32` and either qualify the names or call them as methods:
--! `Int32.add a b`, `a.add b` and `a ▷ Int32.add b` are the same call.
--!
--! **The arithmetic operators do not work on it, and that is the point.**
--! `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `//` and `^` are `number`-kinded — `Int` and
--! `Float` — and `Int32` is neither, so `a + b` on two of them does not
--! compile. Write `add a b`. An operator that read like `Int`'s would hide
--! the one thing this type exists to say, which is that its arithmetic
--! wraps. `==`, `≠`, `<`, `≤`, `>` and `≥` DO work: `eq` and `compare`
--! below are this module's own well-known methods
--! (`static-dispatch-spike.md` §3.3), so an `Int32` is also a `Dict` key, a
--! `Set` member and sortable by `List.sort`, and a record or custom type
--! holding one derives its `eq` and `compare` from these. The order is
--! SIGNED: `minValue < zero < maxValue`.
--!
--! **There is no `Int32` literal.** Write `fromInt 7`, or `fromInt
--! 0xdeadbeef` for a bit pattern — `fromInt` truncates to 32 bits, so a
--! literal past `maxValue` is its low 32 bits and never an error.
--!
--! Argument order is subject first, so `x.shiftLeft 5` and
--! `x ▷ Int32.shiftLeft 5` both read as "shift x left by 5".
--!
--! The machine operations and the two boundaries are written over `Js`
--! (`boundary.md` §4.2, `plans/core-in-beni.md`), and nothing here is
--! `foreign` but the type:
--!
--!   - `fromInt` `toInt` `toUnsignedInt` — the number/`Int32` boundary,
--!     which is a truncation (`n | 0`) in one direction and a
--!     reinterpretation in the other.
--!   - `add` `sub` `mul` `div` `rem` `mod` — wrapping arithmetic. `mul` is
--!     `Math.imul`, which is the whole reason this module exists.
--!   - `and` `or` `xor` `shiftLeft` `shiftRight` `shiftRightZero` — the
--!     native bit operators, which are already exactly 32-bit.
--!
--! An `Int32` IS a JavaScript number in range, so `toInt` reads one as an
--! `Int` for nothing, and `Js.to (Js.from n)` reads an `Int` already in
--! range as an `Int32` for nothing; `fromInt` is the `| 0` that brings one
--! into range. A saturated call of `fromInt`, `toInt`, `toUnsignedInt`,
--! `add`, `sub` or a bit operation is written in place as the operator it
--! is (`backend.md` §4, *Arithmetic is an operator*); the declarations below
--! are what a call passing one as a value reaches.
--!
--! Everything else — `neg`, `complement`, `rotateLeft`, `rotateRight`, the
--! four constants, `eq` and `compare` — is written over those, so it is
--! type-checked, eliminable and documented by the examples below.

import Js


--| A signed 32-bit integer, in two's-complement. Its arithmetic wraps
--| rather than losing precision, which is the difference between it and
--| `Int`.
--|
--|     toInt (fromInt 7) == 7
--|
--| Two `Int32`s are equal when they are the same 32-bit value, and they are
--| ordered as signed integers.
pub equatable foreign type Int32


--| Truncate an `Int` to 32 bits, keeping the low bits. This is the only way
--| to make an `Int32`, and it is total: every `Int` has an answer.
--|
--|     toInt (fromInt 7) == 7
--|     toInt (fromInt 0xdeadbeef) == -559038737
--|     toInt (fromInt 4294967296) == 0
--|     toInt (fromInt 2147483648) == -2147483648
--|     toInt (fromInt (-2147483649)) == 2147483647
--|
--| A value that is not a whole number in range is brought into range the
--| same way JavaScript's `| 0` does, so nothing here can fail: a fraction
--| is truncated towards zero, and a NaN or an infinity is `zero`.
--|
--|     toInt (fromInt (truncate 3.7)) == 3
pub fromInt : Int → Int32
fromInt n = Js.to (Js.from (Js.bitOr n 0))


--| Read an `Int32` back as an `Int`, SIGNED — so a value with the top bit
--| set comes back negative.
--|
--|     toInt (fromInt 0x7fffffff) == 2147483647
--|     toInt (fromInt 0x80000000) == -2147483648
--|     toInt (fromInt 0xffffffff) == -1
--|
--| This never loses anything: every 32-bit value fits an `Int` exactly.
pub toInt : Int32 → Int
toInt x = Js.to (Js.from x)


--| Read an `Int32` back as an `Int`, UNSIGNED — always in 0 to 4294967295.
--|
--|     toUnsignedInt (fromInt 0x7fffffff) == 2147483647
--|     toUnsignedInt (fromInt 0x80000000) == 2147483648
--|     toUnsignedInt (fromInt 0xffffffff) == 4294967295
--|     toUnsignedInt (fromInt (-1)) == 4294967295
--|
--| Hash functions, checksums and binary formats are published in unsigned
--| terms, so this is what you print a result with when you want to compare
--| it against someone else's test vectors.
pub toUnsignedInt : Int32 → Int
toUnsignedInt x = Js.shiftRightZero (toInt x) 0


--| Add, wrapping at 32 bits.
--|
--|     toInt (add (fromInt 1) (fromInt 2)) == 3
--|     add maxValue one == minValue
--|     add minValue (fromInt (-1)) == maxValue
pub add : Int32, Int32 → Int32
add a b = fromInt (toInt a + toInt b)


--| Subtract, wrapping at 32 bits.
--|
--|     toInt (sub (fromInt 3) (fromInt 1)) == 2
--|     sub minValue one == maxValue
--|     sub maxValue (fromInt (-1)) == minValue
pub sub : Int32, Int32 → Int32
sub a b = fromInt (toInt a - toInt b)


--| Multiply, wrapping at 32 bits — the operation `Int` cannot fake.
--|
--|     toInt (mul (fromInt 6) (fromInt 7)) == 42
--|
--| The low 32 bits of the true product, always. The two cases below are the
--| ones that tell a correct implementation from a naive `(a * b) | 0`: the
--| true products are 4611686014132420609 and 121932631112635269, both past
--| 2^53, so the double has already lost the low bits the answer is made of,
--| and the naive form answers 0 and -67153024 instead.
--|
--|     toInt (mul maxValue maxValue) == 1
--|     toInt (mul (fromInt 123456789) (fromInt 987654321)) == -67153019
--|     mul minValue (fromInt (-1)) == minValue
pub mul : Int32, Int32 → Int32
mul a b = Js.pure λ() → Js.to (Js.call (Js.global "Math") "imul" [ Js.from a, Js.from b ])


--| Integer division, truncated towards zero. **Division by zero is `zero`**,
--| as it is for `Basics.idiv`: `Int32` has no infinity and no exception, so
--| returning one would put a value in the type that the type does not hold.
--|
--|     toInt (div (fromInt 7) (fromInt 2)) == 3
--|     toInt (div (fromInt (-7)) (fromInt 2)) == -3
--|     div (fromInt 7) zero == zero
--|
--| `minValue / -1` is the one true overflow of 32-bit division — the answer
--| is 2147483648, which is not an `Int32` — and it wraps like everything
--| else here.
--|
--|     div minValue (fromInt (-1)) == minValue
pub div : Int32, Int32 → Int32
div a b =
    if toInt b == 0 then
        Js.to (Js.from 0)
    else
        fromInt (Js.to (Js.from (Js.to (Js.from a) / Js.to (Js.from b))))


--| Remainder, taking the sign of the DIVIDEND, like `Int.rem`
--| and like `%` in JavaScript. Zero for a zero divisor.
--|
--|     toInt (rem (fromInt 7) (fromInt 3)) == 1
--|     toInt (rem (fromInt (-7)) (fromInt 3)) == -1
--|     rem (fromInt 7) zero == zero
--|     rem minValue (fromInt (-1)) == zero
pub rem : Int32, Int32 → Int32
rem a b =
    if toInt b == 0 then
        Js.to (Js.from 0)
    else
        fromInt (Js.rem (toInt a) (toInt b))


--| Modulus, taking the sign of the DIVISOR, like `Int.mod`. Zero for a
--| zero divisor. This is the one you want for wrapping an index into a
--| table.
--|
--|     toInt (mod (fromInt 7) (fromInt 3)) == 1
--|     toInt (mod (fromInt (-7)) (fromInt 3)) == 2
--|     mod (fromInt 7) zero == zero
pub mod : Int32, Int32 → Int32
mod a b =
    if toInt b == 0 then
        Js.to (Js.from 0)
    else
        fromInt (Js.rem (Js.rem (toInt a) (toInt b) + toInt b) (toInt b))


--| Bitwise and.
--|
--|     toInt (and (fromInt 0xff00ff00) (fromInt 0x0f0f0f0f)) == 0x0f000f00
--|     and (fromInt 0xdeadbeef) zero == zero
pub and : Int32, Int32 → Int32
and a b = Js.to (Js.from (Js.bitAnd (toInt a) (toInt b)))


--| Bitwise or.
--|
--|     toUnsignedInt (or (fromInt 0xff00ff00) (fromInt 0x0f0f0f0f)) == 0xff0fff0f
--|     or (fromInt 0xdeadbeef) zero == fromInt 0xdeadbeef
pub or : Int32, Int32 → Int32
or a b = Js.to (Js.from (Js.bitOr (toInt a) (toInt b)))


--| Bitwise exclusive or.
--|
--|     toUnsignedInt (xor (fromInt 0xff00ff00) (fromInt 0x0f0f0f0f)) == 0xf00ff00f
--|     xor (fromInt 0xdeadbeef) (fromInt 0xdeadbeef) == zero
pub xor : Int32, Int32 → Int32
xor a b = Js.to (Js.from (Js.bitXor (toInt a) (toInt b)))


--| Shift left, filling with zeros. **The count is taken modulo 32**, which
--| is what JavaScript's `<<` does with it: only its low five bits are read,
--| so a count of 32 is a shift of 0 and a count of -1 is a shift of 31.
--|
--|     toInt (shiftLeft one 4) == 16
--|     shiftLeft one 31 == minValue
--|     shiftLeft one 32 == one
--|     shiftLeft one 33 == fromInt 2
pub shiftLeft : Int32, Int → Int32
shiftLeft x count = Js.to (Js.from (Js.shiftLeft (toInt x) count))


--| Shift right, ARITHMETIC: the sign bit is copied into the top, so a
--| negative number stays negative. The count is taken modulo 32, as above.
--|
--|     toInt (shiftRight (fromInt 16) 4) == 1
--|     toInt (shiftRight (fromInt (-16)) 4) == -1
--|     shiftRight (fromInt (-1)) 31 == fromInt (-1)
pub shiftRight : Int32, Int → Int32
shiftRight x count = Js.to (Js.from (Js.shiftRight (toInt x) count))


--| Shift right, LOGICAL: zeros are shifted into the top, so the result of a
--| non-zero shift is never negative. The count is taken modulo 32, as
--| above.
--|
--|     toInt (shiftRightZero (fromInt 16) 4) == 1
--|     toInt (shiftRightZero (fromInt (-16)) 4) == 268435455
--|     toInt (shiftRightZero (fromInt (-1)) 31) == 1
--|
--| This is JavaScript's `>>>`, whose result is unsigned; it is brought back
--| into signed 32-bit range here so that it stays an `Int32`, and a shift
--| of 0 is therefore the identity rather than a conversion.
--|
--|     shiftRightZero (fromInt (-1)) 0 == fromInt (-1)
pub shiftRightZero : Int32, Int → Int32
shiftRightZero x count = fromInt (Js.shiftRightZero (toInt x) count)


--| Zero.
--|
--|     toInt zero == 0
pub zero : Int32
zero = fromInt 0


--| One.
--|
--|     toInt one == 1
pub one : Int32
one = fromInt 1


--| The smallest `Int32`, -2147483648. There is no `Int32` whose `neg` or
--| `abs` this is, which is the two's-complement asymmetry and not a defect.
--|
--|     toInt minValue == -2147483648
--|     neg minValue == minValue
pub minValue : Int32
minValue = fromInt (-2147483648)


--| The largest `Int32`, 2147483647.
--|
--|     toInt maxValue == 2147483647
--|     add maxValue one == minValue
pub maxValue : Int32
maxValue = fromInt 2147483647


--| Negate, wrapping. Written as a subtraction from zero, exactly as
--| `Basics.negate` is.
--|
--|     toInt (neg (fromInt 42)) == -42
--|     neg zero == zero
--|     neg minValue == minValue
pub neg : Int32 → Int32
neg n = sub zero n


--| Flip every bit — JavaScript's `~`, written as an exclusive or with all
--| ones because that is what it is.
--|
--|     toInt (complement zero) == -1
--|     toInt (complement (fromInt 0x0f0f0f0f)) == -252645136
--|     complement (complement (fromInt 12345)) == fromInt 12345
pub complement : Int32 → Int32
complement n = xor n (fromInt (-1))


--| Rotate left: the bits shifted off the top come back in at the bottom.
--| Rotation is what a hash or a PRNG mixes with, and it is not a shift —
--| nothing is lost.
--|
--|     rotateLeft one 1 == fromInt 2
--|     rotateLeft minValue 1 == one
--|     rotateLeft (fromInt 0x12345678) 8 == fromInt 0x34567812
--|     rotateLeft (fromInt 12345) 0 == fromInt 12345
--|     rotateLeft (fromInt 12345) 32 == fromInt 12345
pub rotateLeft : Int32, Int → Int32
rotateLeft n count = or (shiftLeft n count) (shiftRightZero n (32 - count))


--| Rotate right, the mirror of `rotateLeft`.
--|
--|     rotateRight (fromInt 2) 1 == one
--|     rotateRight one 1 == minValue
--|     rotateRight (fromInt 0x12345678) 8 == fromInt 0x78123456
--|     rotateRight (fromInt 12345) 0 == fromInt 12345
pub rotateRight : Int32, Int → Int32
rotateRight n count = or (shiftRightZero n count) (shiftLeft n (32 - count))


--| Whether two `Int32`s are the same 32-bit value. This is what `==` and
--| `≠` on them mean (`static-dispatch-spike.md` §3.3), and it stays
--| callable by name.
--|
--|     eq (fromInt 5) (fromInt 5) == True
--|     eq (fromInt 5) (fromInt 6) == False
--|     eq (fromInt 4294967296) zero == True
pub eq : Int32, Int32 → Bool
eq a b = toInt a == toInt b


--| Compare two `Int32`s as SIGNED integers. This is what `<`, `≤`, `>` and
--| `≥` on them mean, and it is what makes an `Int32` a `Dict` key, a `Set`
--| member and sortable.
--|
--|     compare (fromInt 1) (fromInt 2) == LT
--|     compare (fromInt 2) (fromInt 2) == EQ
--|     compare minValue maxValue == LT
--|     compare (fromInt (-1)) zero == LT
--|
--| Signed, not unsigned: `fromInt 0xffffffff` is -1 and sorts below `zero`,
--| however you print it. Sort a hash table by `toUnsignedInt` when you want
--| the other order.
pub compare : Int32, Int32 → Order
compare a b = Basics.compare (toInt a) (toInt b)
